menu "sys_uart"

    config SYS_UART_TASK_PRIO
        int "RX task priority"
        default 5
        range 1 24
        help
          Priority of the task that reads the console UART (and USB-Serial-JTAG)
          and hands each frame (COBS, CRC16 checked) to the provider's user.

    config SYS_UART_TASK_STACK
        int "RX task stack (bytes)"
        default 4096
        range 2048 16384
        help
          The frame buffers are static, not on this stack. The session's
          handling of a hello or a lost child runs on it (a subtree list).

    config SYS_UART_DRIVER_RX_BUF
        int "Driver RX buffer (bytes)"
        default 1024
        range 256 8192
        help
          RX buffer of the UART (and USB-Serial-JTAG) driver installed on the
          console. Must be larger than the UART hardware FIFO (128 bytes).

    config SYS_UART_TX_QUEUE
        int "TX queue (bytes)"
        default 8192
        range 1024 32768
        help
          Frames waiting for the TX task (answers, stream frames); each takes
          its length rounded to 4 + 8 bytes. Urgent frames have a queue of
          their own, written first. Larger than the connector queue
          (CONFIG_SYS_DATA_CONNECTOR_QUEUE_SIZE), so a burst of stream frames
          fills that one first and its producer is refused (and resends), not
          lost here unseen. Multiple of 4.

    config SYS_UART_TX_TASK_PRIO
        int "TX task priority"
        default 2
        range 1 24
        help
          The task that encodes the queued frames and writes them. Low: the
          console is slow, and nothing waits for it but the queue.

    config SYS_UART_TX_TASK_STACK
        int "TX task stack (bytes)"
        default 3072
        range 2048 16384

    config SYS_UART_SEND_WAIT_MS
        int "Send wait (ms)"
        default 1000
        range 10 10000
        help
          How long a send without NOWAIT (an answer) waits for room in the TX
          queue, then ERR_PROV_BUSY; a NOWAIT send (a stream frame) is
          ERR_PROV_BUSY at once. Also how long a frame waits for the
          USB-Serial-JTAG host.

endmenu
